# Applied after mainline/j4mainline.fragment, which is kept verbatim.
#
# Everything here exists because the pinned tree and the fragment disagree, and
# the disagreement was found by fragment-audit.py rather than by a phone that
# quietly had no display.

# The J4+ panel is an S6D7AT0B01 (see androidboot cmdline:
# ss_dsi_panel_S6D7AT0B01_PM6003XB1_HDP). The fragment asks for
# DRM_PANEL_SAMSUNG_S6D7AT0B01, which is the generated driver carried on branch
# pmos-j4primelte-20260927 (ci/j4primelte/mainline/panel-samsung-s6d7at0b01.c)
# together with its own Kconfig entry. The pinned mainline tree has no such
# symbol; it has DRM_PANEL_SAMSUNG_S6D7AA0, the same controller family.
#
# So: use the in-tree driver. If it does not drive this panel, the next step is
# to bring the generated driver over as a patch, not to invent a symbol name.
+ DRM_PANEL_SAMSUNG_S6D7AA0
# The line above is the declaration that makes the earlier unknown symbol
# explicit instead of silent.
! SUPERSEDES DRM_PANEL_SAMSUNG_S6D7AT0B01

# systemd's cgroup v2 device controller is CONFIG_CGROUP_BPF, and that symbol is
# "depends on BPF_SYSCALL".
#
# The mainline fragment disables BPF_SYSCALL (line 222) because that bring-up
# build did not want eBPF. That is no longer tenable: with BPF_SYSCALL off,
# scripts/config accepts CGROUP_BPF, olddefconfig silently drops it, the kernel
# compiles, and the audit reports CGROUP_BPF as ABSENT. Observed on run
# 43380010613 - compiled, then blocked by the audit.
#
# This file is applied after the mainline fragment, so this wins.
+ BPF_SYSCALL
+ CGROUP_BPF
